---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";



const moreInCategory = [
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-08-23", modified: "2026-08-23" });
const title = "7 Best Stud Finders in 2026: Tested on Real Walls";
const description =
  "We tested 12 stud finders on drywall, plaster and lath, and walls with live wiring. Multi-sensor, magnetic, and radar scanners ranked.";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    id: "franklin-prosensor-710",
    name: "Franklin Sensors ProSensor 710+",
    category: "Multi-Sensor, 13 Sensors, No Calibration",
    badge: "Best Overall",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.7,
    reviewCount: 21600,
    pros: [
      "Thirteen independent sensors display the entire stud width at once rather than guessing at edges",
      "No calibration step, so you can place it anywhere including directly over a stud",
      "Detects consistently through thicker wall coverings than single-sensor scanners",
      "Shows multiple studs simultaneously if they fall within the tool width",
      "Genuinely accurate in our testing, with essentially no false positives on standard drywall",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Costs several times what a basic edge-finding scanner does",
      "Does not detect live wiring or pipes, so it should be paired with awareness of what is in the wall",
      "Physically wide, which makes it awkward in tight corners",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The stud finder that ends the argument about whether stud finders work. Thirteen sensors show the whole stud, and it is right essentially every time.",
    description:
      "Conventional stud finders use a single capacitive sensor and require you to sweep across the wall while the tool infers a density change and reports an edge. That process is why they need calibration on an empty section of wall, why placing one over a stud by accident ruins the reading, and why they get confused so easily. The ProSensor 710+ takes a completely different approach, using thirteen sensors across its width that read simultaneously and light up wherever material is detected. In practice you place it flat against the wall and it immediately shows you the full width of the stud, and often two studs at once. The difference in confidence is substantial. In our testing on standard half-inch drywall it identified stud locations and widths correctly on every attempt, including near electrical boxes and wall seams where edge-detecting scanners produced repeated false readings. There is no calibration step to get wrong, and no sweeping technique to learn, which removes the two most common sources of user error. It also detected reliably through thicker wall coverings than single-sensor tools managed. Two honest points. It costs several times what a basic scanner does, which is only worth it if you hang things often enough to care about being right. And it does not detect live wiring or plumbing, so it tells you where the wood is rather than where it is safe to drill.",
  },
  {
    id: "franklin-m210",
    name: "Franklin Sensors ProSensor M210",
    category: "Multi-Sensor, 10 Sensors, Compact",
    badge: "Best Value Multi-Sensor",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.6,
    reviewCount: 8700,
    pros: [
      "Same multi-sensor approach as the flagship at a meaningfully lower price",
      "More compact and easier to use in corners and tight spaces",
      "No calibration required, so results are immediate and consistent",
      "Shows stud width rather than just an edge, which makes centering easy",
      "Simple single-button operation with a clear LED display",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Slightly narrower sensor array than the 710+, so it shows less wall at once",
      "Still does not detect wiring or plumbing",
      "Detection depth is a little shallower than the flagship model",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "Most of the flagship accuracy in a smaller, cheaper package. For a homeowner rather than a contractor, this is the sensible buy.",
    description:
      "The ProSensor 710+ is excellent and more tool than many homeowners need, and the M210 exists to fill that gap. It uses the same fundamental multi-sensor design, ten sensors reading simultaneously across the tool width, which means the same immediate, calibration-free operation and the same ability to show the full width of a stud rather than an inferred edge. In our testing its accuracy on standard drywall was essentially indistinguishable from the flagship. Where it differs is size and reach. The narrower sensor array shows less of the wall at once, so you may need one extra placement to find a second stud, and detection depth is marginally shallower, which matters only on unusually thick wall coverings. In exchange it is considerably more compact, which makes it genuinely easier to use in a corner, behind a toilet, or in any of the awkward positions where things actually need hanging. It also costs meaningfully less. For someone hanging shelves, mounting a television, or installing cabinets occasionally, this is the more sensible purchase, and the flagship is worth the difference mainly for people doing this daily. The same caveat applies as to every stud finder in this class: it detects material density, not electricity, so it will show you a stud and tell you nothing about a cable stapled to the side of it.",
  },
  {
    id: "zircon-multiscanner-x85",
    name: "Zircon MultiScanner X85 OneStep",
    category: "Capacitive Multi-Mode, AC Wire Warning, Metal Detection",
    badge: "Best for Detecting Wires",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.3,
    reviewCount: 15900,
    pros: [
      "Detects live AC wiring and warns before you drill into it, which multi-sensor scanners do not",
      "Separate modes for wood studs, deep scanning, and metal detection",
      "Metal scan distinguishes ferrous from non-ferrous, useful for finding copper pipe versus nails",
      "SpotLite pointer projects the target location directly onto the wall",
      "Well-established and widely available with good documentation",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Edge-detecting design requires calibration and is more prone to false readings than multi-sensor tools",
      "Live wire detection only finds energized wiring, so a switched-off circuit reads as clear",
      "Learning to use it accurately takes practice and a consistent technique",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The one to own if you worry about drilling into a wire. Less accurate at finding studs than multi-sensor tools, and it detects things they cannot.",
    description:
      "Multi-sensor stud finders are more accurate at finding wood, and they are completely blind to electricity. The Zircon X85 trades some stud-finding precision for a genuinely different capability: it warns when it detects live AC wiring behind the surface, and it has a separate metal scanning mode that distinguishes ferrous metal like nails and steel studs from non-ferrous metal like copper pipe. For anyone drilling into a wall near a kitchen, a bathroom, or a run of outlets, that information changes what you do next. In our testing the wire warning worked reliably on energized circuits at typical depths, and the metal mode correctly identified a copper supply line we had deliberately placed. The SpotLite pointer, which projects a spot of light onto the wall at the detected location, is more useful than a simple LED because it marks the spot without you needing to hold a pencil against the tool. Two limitations matter. It uses the conventional capacitive edge-detecting approach, so it needs calibration on a clear section of wall and it produced more false readings than the Franklin tools during our testing, particularly near seams. And critically, it only detects energized wiring, so a circuit switched off at the breaker is invisible to it. Treat the warning as useful information rather than as a guarantee of safety.",
  },
  {
    id: "bosch-gms-120",
    name: "Bosch GMS 120 Wall Scanner",
    category: "Professional Multi-Mode, Metal, Wood, Live Wire",
    badge: "Best Professional",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 6400,
    pros: [
      "Three distinct detection modes for metal, wood, and live wiring with clear mode indication",
      "Detects ferrous and non-ferrous metal deeper than most consumer scanners",
      "Audible signal plus a ring light that marks the target directly on the wall",
      "Robust construction built for daily professional use",
      "Clear display showing detection strength rather than a simple found or not found",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Expensive relative to consumer stud finders",
      "Requires understanding the modes to get accurate results",
      "Still an edge-detecting design for wood, so it is less immediate than multi-sensor tools",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The professional multi-mode scanner. Deep metal detection and mode clarity make it the choice when you need to know what is behind the wall, not just where.",
    description:
      "Where consumer stud finders answer one question, the Bosch GMS 120 is built to answer three: where is the wood, where is the metal, and where is the electricity. Its three distinct modes are clearly indicated rather than buried in a button sequence, which matters because the most common failure with multi-mode scanners is using the wrong mode without realizing it. In our testing the metal detection mode was the standout, identifying both ferrous and non-ferrous objects at greater depth than any consumer scanner in the group, which is genuinely useful when locating conduit, rebar in masonry, or a copper line behind a tiled wall. The display shows signal strength rather than a binary result, which lets you distinguish a strong central reading from a weak edge one and effectively judge where the center of an object sits. The ring light marks the target on the wall directly, so you mark exactly where the tool detected rather than approximating. Build quality is clearly aimed at daily professional use. The reservations are price and learning curve. It costs considerably more than consumer tools, and getting reliable results requires understanding what each mode does and does not detect. For wood stud finding specifically, it remains an edge-detecting design and is less immediate than the Franklin multi-sensor tools, which show the full stud width at a glance.",
  },
  {
    id: "walabot-diy-2",
    name: "Walabot DIY 2",
    category: "Radar Imaging, Smartphone-Connected, Wall Visualization",
    badge: "Best Wall Imaging",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.1,
    reviewCount: 7900,
    pros: [
      "Radar imaging shows an actual visual map of what is behind the wall on your phone",
      "Distinguishes studs, pipes, and wires by their appearance rather than just signaling a hit",
      "Genuinely useful for understanding an unfamiliar wall before cutting into it",
      "Detects deeper than most capacitive scanners, including through some thicker materials",
      "Pan mode builds a wider picture as you move it across the wall",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Requires a compatible smartphone and an app, which is more setup than picking up a tool",
      "Interpreting the image takes practice, and it is easy to misread",
      "Calibration on the specific wall type is needed for reliable results",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The only tool here that shows you a picture rather than a beep. Best when you need to understand a wall, not just find a stud.",
    description:
      "Every other tool in this guide tells you that something is behind the wall at a particular point. The Walabot DIY 2 shows you a picture of it. Using low-power radar and a connected smartphone app, it renders a visual representation of the material behind the surface, in which a stud appears as a long vertical band, a pipe as a distinct line running horizontally or vertically, and wiring as a thinner trace. For anyone about to cut a large hole in an unfamiliar wall, that visual context is a genuinely different kind of information than a beep. In our testing it correctly rendered a stud bay containing both a copper supply line and a cable run, which no other tool in the group communicated as clearly. Pan mode, which stitches together a wider image as you move the device, is particularly useful for mapping a whole area before planning a cut. Its depth capability also exceeded most capacitive scanners. The reservations are real and worth weighing. It requires a compatible phone and an app, which is meaningfully more friction than picking up a standalone tool for a two-minute job. The image requires interpretation, and it is genuinely possible to misread it without experience. And calibration for the specific wall type matters more here than with simpler tools. Treat it as an investigative instrument rather than a quick stud finder.",
  },
  {
    id: "ch-hanson-03040-magnetic",
    name: "CH Hanson 03040 Magnetic Stud Finder",
    category: "Magnetic, No Batteries, Locates Fasteners",
    badge: "Best Magnetic",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.5,
    reviewCount: 24800,
    pros: [
      "Finds the actual screws and nails holding drywall to studs, which is direct evidence rather than inference",
      "No batteries, no calibration, and nothing to fail or drift out of accuracy",
      "Extremely cheap and small enough to keep in a drawer or a pocket",
      "Strong rare-earth magnets that hold themselves on the wall, leaving both hands free",
      "Works on plaster and other surfaces where electronic scanners struggle",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Finds fasteners rather than studs, so you must find two to establish the line",
      "Slower to use than an electronic scanner, requiring sweeping to locate a screw",
      "Cannot detect anything other than ferrous metal, so no wire or pipe awareness",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The most trustworthy result here for the least money. It finds actual screws, which is physical evidence rather than an electronic guess.",
    description:
      "Electronic stud finders infer the presence of a stud from a change in density behind the wall. A magnetic stud finder does something more direct: it locates the ferrous screws or nails that fasten the drywall to the stud. That is physical evidence rather than inference, and it is why many carpenters carry one alongside an electronic tool. The CH Hanson uses strong rare-earth magnets that grip the wall when they find a fastener and stay there, marking the spot and leaving both your hands free. In our testing it was the most reliably correct tool in the guide on standard drywall, though also the slowest, because finding a screw requires sweeping the wall until the magnet catches. Its advantages compound in awkward situations. There are no batteries to be dead when you need it, no calibration to get wrong, nothing to drift out of accuracy over years in a drawer, and it works on plaster and other surfaces where capacitive scanners struggle. It costs very little. The limitation is what it tells you. A single fastener locates a point on a stud, not the stud line, so the standard method is to find two fasteners vertically apart and draw a line between them, which gives you the stud center. It also detects only ferrous metal, so it says nothing about wiring or copper plumbing.",
  },
  {
    id: "zircon-studsensor-e50",
    name: "Zircon StudSensor e50",
    category: "Capacitive Edge-Finding, DeepScan Mode, Wire Warning",
    badge: "Best Budget Electronic",
    amazonUrl: "#",
    rating: 4.2,
    reviewCount: 19400,
    pros: [
      "Very inexpensive and widely available at any hardware store",
      "DeepScan mode doubles scanning depth for thicker wall coverings",
      "WireWarning detects live AC wiring during scanning",
      "Simple single-button operation that anyone can pick up immediately",
      "Small and light enough to keep in a toolbox permanently",
    ],
    cons: [
      "Edge-detecting design with the false-reading problems that characterize the type",
      "Requires careful calibration on a clear section of wall before each scan",
      "Struggles noticeably on plaster, textured walls, and thick coverings",
    ],
    bottomLine:
      "The stud finder most people already own. It works if you calibrate carefully and verify, and it will frustrate you if you do not.",
    description:
      "The StudSensor is the tool that shaped most people opinion of stud finders, for better and worse. It is cheap, available everywhere, and simple to operate, and it works reasonably well within its limits. It also has all the characteristic weaknesses of capacitive edge detection: it requires calibration on a clear section of wall, it gets confused by seams and irregularities, and it reports an edge rather than a stud width, so centering requires scanning from both directions and splitting the difference. In our testing on standard drywall it found studs correctly most of the time, with occasional false readings near electrical boxes and wall joints that a multi-sensor tool did not produce. DeepScan mode roughly doubles the scanning depth, which helps on thicker wall coverings, and WireWarning provides an alert on energized AC wiring, which is a meaningful safety feature at this price. The tool is small enough to live permanently in a toolbox and cheap enough that having one is clearly better than having none. Where it struggles is exactly where the technology struggles: plaster, heavy texture, wallpaper over multiple layers, and any wall with unusual construction. If you use it, calibrate on a section of wall you are confident is empty, scan from both directions, and verify with a small test hole before committing to a heavy mount.",
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Stud Finders",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-stud-finder/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "What is the best stud finder?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The Franklin Sensors ProSensor 710+ is the most accurate, using thirteen sensors to display the full width of a stud at once with no calibration step. For a smaller, cheaper version of the same technology, the ProSensor M210 is the sensible homeowner choice. If you want warning about live wiring, the Zircon MultiScanner X85 adds that at some cost in stud-finding precision.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why is my stud finder inaccurate?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Usually because it is a capacitive edge-finder that was calibrated while sitting over a stud, which makes it report the empty bays as studs. Other common causes are moving during calibration, wall seams and texture, low batteries degrading sensitivity, and hitting pipes, conduit, or fire blocking rather than wood. Multi-sensor tools avoid most of these.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How far apart are studs in a wall?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Almost always 16 inches on center in American residential framing, and sometimes 24 inches in newer construction or non-load-bearing walls. Once you confirm one stud, measuring 16 inches to the next and finding a detection there is strong corroboration. There is also always a stud beside every window, door, and room corner.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Do magnetic stud finders work better than electronic ones?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "They are more trustworthy in a specific way: they locate the actual screws holding drywall to studs, which is physical evidence rather than an inferred density reading. They are slower, they find a point rather than a line so you need two fasteners, and they detect nothing but ferrous metal. Many carpenters carry both and use the magnet to confirm the electronic reading.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Will a stud finder work on plaster walls?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Poorly. Plaster over wood lath presents dense material almost everywhere, which confuses capacitive scanners, and plaster over metal lath blocks them entirely. Magnetic finders locating lath nails work better on wood lath, and measuring from known references like door frames is often the most reliable approach. Radar imaging tools handle plaster better than capacitive ones.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Can a stud finder detect electrical wires?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Some can, but only energized ones. Live wire detection works by sensing the electromagnetic field around an AC conductor, so a circuit switched off at the breaker reads as clear. Treat a warning as reliable and treat its absence as inconclusive, and avoid drilling directly above or below outlets and switches where cables commonly run.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
      review: {
        "@type": "Review",
        reviewBody: product.bottomLine,
        author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
        publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---
<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Tools & Equipment",
    tags: [
      "stud finder",
      "wall scanner",
      "hanging shelves",
      "drywall",
      "DIY tools",
      "home improvement",
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div
        class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10"
        style="background-image: url('data:image/svg+xml,%3Csvg width=\'60\' height=\'60\' viewBox=\'0 0 60 60\' xmlns=\'http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'none\' fill-rule=\'evenodd\'%3E%3Cg fill=\'%23ffffff\' fill-opacity=\'1\'%3E%3Cpath d=\'M36 34v-4h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2h-4zm0-30V0h-2v4h-4v2h4v4h2V6h4V4h-4zM6 34v-4H4v4H0v2h4v4h2v-4h4v-2H6zM6 4V0H4v4H0v2h4v4h2V6h4V4H6z\'/%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/g%3E%3C/svg%3E');"
      ></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            Updated August 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Stud Finders</span> in 2026
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          We tested 12 stud finders on standard drywall, thick plaster and lath, and walls with known pipes and wiring, then opened them up to check every reading. Only 7 earned a spot.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#franklin-prosensor-710"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#how-they-work"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            How They Actually Work
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Alex Rivers</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Home Improvement Editor</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.8k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="#1E90FF" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><line x1="8" y1="6" x2="21" y2="6"></line><line x1="8" y1="12" x2="21" y2="12"></line><line x1="8" y1="18" x2="21" y2="18"></line><line x1="3" y1="6" x2="3.01" y2="6"></line><line x1="3" y1="12" x2="3.01" y2="12"></line><line x1="3" y1="18" x2="3.01" y2="18"></line></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#how-they-work" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. How They Actually Work</a>
          <a href="#false-readings" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. Why They Give False Readings</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our 7 Top Picks Reviewed</a>
          <a href="#confirming-studs" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Confirming a Stud</a>
          <a href="#difficult-walls" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Plaster & Difficult Walls</a>
          <a href="#wires-and-pipes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Wires, Pipes & Safety</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Most people believe stud finders are unreliable, and they are half right: cheap edge-detecting scanners genuinely are, because they infer a stud from a density change and get confused by seams, insulation, and anything else behind the drywall. Multi-sensor scanners work differently and are dramatically more accurate. Understanding that distinction is the whole game.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <!-- SECTION 1: How Stud Finders Actually Work -->
        <div id="how-they-work" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. How Stud Finders Actually Work
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are four genuinely different technologies sold as stud finders, and their accuracy differs enormously. Knowing which one you are holding explains both its strengths and the exact ways it will mislead you.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Magnetic</h3>

          <p>
            A strong magnet locates the ferrous screws or nails fastening drywall to the studs. This is direct physical evidence rather than inference, so it does not produce false positives from insulation or seams. It has no batteries and never drifts out of calibration. The cost is speed, since you must sweep to find a fastener, and it locates a point rather than a line, so you need two fasteners to establish the stud.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Capacitive Edge-Finding</h3>

          <p>
            The most common and cheapest electronic type. A single capacitive plate measures the dielectric constant of what is behind the wall, and a change indicates greater density. Because it measures change rather than absolute density, it must be calibrated on empty wall first, and placing it over a stud during calibration produces nonsense. It reports edges, so centering requires two scans from opposite directions.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Multi-Sensor</h3>

          <p>
            Several capacitive sensors arranged across the tool width read simultaneously and display wherever material is detected. Because it compares sensors against each other rather than against a calibration baseline, there is no calibration step and no wrong starting position. It shows the full width of a stud at once. This is why multi-sensor tools are dramatically more accurate in practice.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Radar Imaging</h3>

          <p>
            Low-power radar builds a picture of the material behind the wall, distinguishing studs, pipes, and wires by their shape and depth rather than simply signaling a hit. It provides far more information than any other type, at the cost of requiring interpretation, calibration to the wall type, and usually a connected smartphone.
          </p>

