        <div id="false-readings" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Why Stud Finders Give False Readings
          </h2>

          <p>
            The reputation stud finders have for being unreliable is mostly earned by one technology used incorrectly. Here is what actually causes bad readings and how to avoid each.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Calibrating over a stud.</strong> Capacitive edge-finders establish a baseline when you press the button, and if that starting point happens to be over a stud, the tool treats stud density as normal and reports the empty bays as anomalies. This produces readings that are exactly wrong, and it is the single most common failure. Multi-sensor tools eliminate it entirely because they have no calibration step.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Moving during calibration.</strong> Many scanners require you to hold still while the tool initializes. Starting to sweep too early corrupts the baseline. Press, wait for the indicator, then move.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wall irregularities.</strong> Drywall seams with joint compound, patched areas, heavy texture, wallpaper, and paneling all change the dielectric behavior the tool measures. Any of them can produce a density reading indistinguishable from a stud.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Things that are not studs.</strong> Plumbing, electrical conduit, cable runs, fire blocking, metal corner bead, and dense insulation all read as material to a capacitive scanner. A hit is evidence that something is there, not evidence that it is wood.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Moisture and hand position.</strong> Capacitive sensing is affected by the dielectric of nearby material, which includes your own hand. Gripping the tool differently than during calibration, or scanning a wall with moisture behind it, both shift readings.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Batteries.</strong> A low battery degrades sensitivity long before the tool warns you or stops working, producing intermittent and inconsistent readings that feel like the tool has become unreliable. Fresh batteries fix a surprising number of stud finder complaints.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-amber-50 border border-amber-200 rounded-2xl p-6 my-8">
            <p class="text-sm font-black text-amber-800 mb-2">Always Verify Before You Commit</p>
            <p class="text-sm text-amber-700">
              Whatever tool you use, confirm before hanging anything heavy. Two independent checks are worth the thirty seconds: scan from both directions and see whether the readings agree, and confirm with a magnetic finder that there is a fastener on the line. For a heavy mount, a small test hole with a thin bit inside the mounting footprint costs nothing to patch and tells you definitively whether there is wood there.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>

