        <!-- SECTION 4: Confirming a Stud Without Relying on the Tool -->
        <div id="confirming-studs" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Confirming a Stud Without Relying on the Tool
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most reliable approach combines an electronic reading with physical evidence, and the physical checks take almost no time.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Use standard spacing to predict.</strong> Studs in American residential framing are almost always spaced 16 inches on center, and sometimes 24 inches in newer or non-load-bearing walls. Once you find one confirmed stud, measure 16 inches to find the next, and if your measurement lands on another detected stud, you have strong corroboration for both.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Start from a known reference.</strong> There is always a stud on each side of a window and a door, and studs typically sit at the corners of a room and beside electrical boxes, since boxes are nailed to studs. Measuring from an outlet is one of the fastest ways to establish a starting point, remembering that the box is beside the stud rather than centered on it.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Confirm with a magnet.</strong> A magnetic finder locates the actual screws holding the drywall. Finding two fasteners vertically apart and drawing a line through them gives you the stud line directly, and it corroborates whatever an electronic tool reported.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Knock, but do not trust it alone.</strong> Tapping the wall produces a duller, higher sound over a stud and a hollower one between. It is a reasonable rough guide and a poor final answer, particularly on plaster or double-layer drywall.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Drill a small test hole where the mount will cover it.</strong> A 1/16-inch bit inside the footprint of the bracket you are installing tells you definitively whether there is wood behind. If you miss, the hole is invisible once mounted and takes seconds to fill anyway. For anything heavy, this is the check worth doing.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Remember the stud is 1.5 inches wide.</strong> A 2x4 presents a face only an inch and a half across, and a lag bolt near the edge can split the wood or barely catch. Find the center rather than the edge, which is exactly the advantage multi-sensor tools provide by showing full width.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 5: Plaster, Lath, and Other Difficult Walls -->
        <div id="difficult-walls" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Plaster, Lath, and Other Difficult Walls
          </h2>

          <p>
            Standard drywall is the easy case. Older and unusual wall construction defeats most electronic scanners, and different techniques become necessary.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plaster and Wood Lath</h3>

          <p>
            Plaster over wood lath is the hardest common wall for a capacitive scanner. The lath itself is wood, running horizontally across the whole wall at close spacing, which means the scanner sees dense material almost everywhere. Multi-sensor tools do better because they compare relative density, but the reliable method in plaster is a magnetic finder locating the nails that fasten lath to studs, or measuring from a known reference like a door frame.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Plaster Over Metal Lath</h3>

          <p>
            Worse still. Metal lath forms a continuous conductive layer that blocks capacitive scanning entirely and saturates metal detection. Magnetic finders are also overwhelmed. Here the practical approaches are measuring from known references, using a radar imaging tool, or drilling small exploratory holes in an inconspicuous area.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Tile and Backer Board</h3>

          <p>
            Tile over cement backer board is dense and thick, exceeding the scanning depth of most consumer tools. Deep scan modes help, and this is a case where a professional scanner with greater depth or a radar imager earns its price. Locating studs before tiling and recording the positions is far easier than finding them afterward.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Paneling and Multiple Layers</h3>

          <p>
            Wood paneling, or drywall installed over old plaster, puts extra thickness between the sensor and the stud. Use a deep scan mode if available, and expect reduced accuracy. Magnetic finders often still work if the outer layer is fastened to the studs.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Metal Stud Framing</h3>

          <p>
            Common in commercial construction and some newer homes. Metal studs are thin and detected easily by any tool with a metal mode, and by magnetic finders. The important difference is fastening: metal studs require self-tapping screws or toggle anchors, and lag bolts intended for wood will not hold.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- SECTION 6: Wires, Pipes, and Not Drilling Into Them -->
        <div id="wires-and-pipes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Wires, Pipes, and Not Drilling Into Them
          </h2>

          <p>
            The stud finder answers where the wood is. Whether it is safe to drill there is a separate question, and it deserves more attention than it usually gets.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Live wire detection has a critical limitation.</strong> Scanners that warn about AC wiring detect the electromagnetic field of an energized conductor. A circuit switched off at the breaker produces no field and reads as clear, which means the absence of a warning is not proof that no wiring is present. Treat a warning as reliable and its absence as inconclusive.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Wiring runs where you would expect it.</strong> Cables typically run vertically between outlets and switches, and horizontally within stud bays a foot or so above the floor or near outlet height. Drilling directly above or below an outlet or switch is statistically the most likely place to hit a cable, and it is worth avoiding regardless of what a scanner says.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Plumbing concentrates in specific walls.</strong> Supply lines and drains run in walls behind and beside kitchens, bathrooms, and laundry areas, and vertically through the wall cavity between floors. A wall that backs onto a bathroom deserves considerably more caution than an interior bedroom wall.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Limit your drilling depth.</strong> Drywall is half an inch thick, so a screw needs to penetrate that plus enough into the stud to hold. Setting a depth stop or a piece of tape on the bit at the depth you actually need prevents driving several inches into a cavity you have not inspected.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Nail plates are a good sign and a hazard.</strong> Code requires metal protective plates where wiring or plumbing passes close to the face of a stud. A metal detection hit on the face of a stud may well be a nail plate protecting something behind it, which is a clear signal to move.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>When you are genuinely unsure, look.</strong> Removing an outlet cover, checking the basement or crawl space below, or looking in the attic above will often reveal exactly where lines run into a wall. Five minutes of investigation is cheaper than a repair.
          </p>
        </div>

