          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best stud finder?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Franklin Sensors ProSensor 710+ is the most accurate, using thirteen sensors to display the full width of a stud at once with no calibration step. For a smaller, cheaper version of the same technology, the ProSensor M210 is the sensible homeowner choice. If you want warning about live wiring, the Zircon MultiScanner X85 adds that at some cost in stud-finding precision.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why is my stud finder inaccurate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Usually because it is a capacitive edge-finder that was calibrated while sitting over a stud, which makes it report the empty bays as studs. Other common causes are moving during calibration, wall seams and texture, low batteries degrading sensitivity, and hitting pipes, conduit, or fire blocking rather than wood. Multi-sensor tools avoid most of these.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far apart are studs in a wall?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Almost always 16 inches on center in American residential framing, and sometimes 24 inches in newer construction or non-load-bearing walls. Once you confirm one stud, measuring 16 inches to the next and finding a detection there is strong corroboration. There is also always a stud beside every window, door, and room corner.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do magnetic stud finders work better than electronic ones?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                They are more trustworthy in a specific way: they locate the actual screws holding drywall to studs, which is physical evidence rather than an inferred density reading. They are slower, they find a point rather than a line so you need two fasteners, and they detect nothing but ferrous metal. Many carpenters carry both and use the magnet to confirm the electronic reading.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a stud finder work on plaster walls?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Poorly. Plaster over wood lath presents dense material almost everywhere, which confuses capacitive scanners, and plaster over metal lath blocks them entirely. Magnetic finders locating lath nails work better on wood lath, and measuring from known references like door frames is often the most reliable approach. Radar imaging tools handle plaster better than capacitive ones.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a stud finder detect electrical wires?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Some can, but only energized ones. Live wire detection works by sensing the electromagnetic field around an AC conductor, so a circuit switched off at the breaker reads as clear. Treat a warning as reliable and treat its absence as inconclusive, and avoid drilling directly above or below outlets and switches where cables commonly run.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
