        <!-- Common Mistakes Section -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Stud Finder Mistakes
          </h2>

          <p>
            Nearly every stud finder complaint traces to one of these, and all four are easy to avoid once you know about them.
          </p>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Calibrating While Sitting Over a Stud</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Capacitive edge-finders set their baseline where you press the button. Do that over a stud and the tool treats stud density as normal, then flags the empty bays instead. The result is readings that are precisely backwards, and it is the single most common cause of a stud finder that seems broken. Multi-sensor tools have no calibration step at all.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming a Detection Means Wood</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Capacitive scanners detect density, not species. Plumbing, conduit, cable runs, fire blocking, metal corner bead, and packed insulation all register. A hit tells you something is there, and driving a lag bolt into a copper supply line because the tool beeped is an expensive way to learn the difference.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Aiming for the Edge Instead of the Center</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 2x4 presents a face only an inch and a half wide, and edge-finding scanners report an edge rather than a center. A fastener placed near the edge can split the stud or barely bite. Scan from both directions, mark both edges, and drill the middle, or use a multi-sensor tool that shows the full width directly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Trusting a Single Reading for a Heavy Mount</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A television, a heavy shelf, or a cabinet coming off the wall is expensive and dangerous. Corroborate with a second method before committing: check that spacing from a confirmed stud is 16 inches, verify with a magnet that a fastener sits on the line, or drill a small test hole inside the mounting footprint where it will be hidden.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

